\section{How a width is chosen}{


The page decides the budget.  The sheet's writable width (paper and
orientation minus the side margins) is divided by the width of one character
in \code{font} at \code{size_half_points}, which gives the number of characters the
listing has to spend.  The gutter columns are subtracted first -- they are
printed too -- and so is every \code{width} you set yourself, because those are
decisions, not proposals.

A gutter costs \code{listing_spec(spacer_rel_width = )} characters, and the
default of \code{1} is a full character each: on a nine-gutter listing that is
close to 6\% of the page.  A fraction is allowed -- \code{spacer_rel_width = 0.25}
gives the hairline divider a hand-tuned listing usually uses.

Each remaining column's \strong{demand} is the largest of four things:
\itemize{
\item the \code{probs} quantile of the display widths of its composed cells -- a
quantile rather than the maximum, so one unusually long value wraps
instead of pushing every other column narrow;
\item the width its header needs to be no more than \code{header_lines} tall;
\item the widest token its header cannot break, below which the header would be
cut mid-word;
\item \code{min_width}.
}

The second is what stops a long label being answered with a very narrow
column.  A header wraps, so it does not need its full length -- but wrapping
a 73-character label into nine characters makes a \strong{ten-line block, printed
at the top of every page}.  \code{header_lines} says how tall a header may
reasonably be, and the label's width divided by it is what the column needs
to get there.  Lower it for a listing whose headers must stay readable;
raise it (or use \code{Inf}) to let the data have the page.

The demands are then scaled to the budget.  Where scaling would take a
column below the width its header needs, the column is raised back to it and
the characters are taken from the columns that have room to spare -- \strong{a
header is never cut mid-word}.  Only if the headers alone cannot fit the
page does that guarantee give way.

Where the data carries no \code{label} attributes, \code{labels} supplies the words,
and the measurement uses those.

Widths are display widths, so a full-width (CJK) glyph counts as two
throughout.
}

